  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The solution to the equation $4\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=3\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x-1$ for ${0}^{\u2218}\\le x<{360}^{\u2218}$ is $x=${{field-1}}${}^{\u2218}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x & =3\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}\u2061x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(-1)=-{90}^{\u2218}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-{90}^{\u2218}$ is not in the given domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{\u2218}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{90}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}={270}^{\u2218}\n$$\n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={270}^{\u2218}+n\\cdot {360}^{\u2218}.\n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<{360}^{\u2218}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that \n$x={270}^{\u2218}$\nis the only solution.",
